<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import VisitsFeed from './lib/VisitsFeed.abide'
import WarehouseExport from './lib/WarehouseExport.abide'
import SalesCsv from './lib/SalesCsv.abide'
import RegionReport from './lib/RegionReport.abide'
import visitsFeedSource from './lib/VisitsFeed.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import warehouseExportSource from './lib/WarehouseExport.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import salesCsvSource from './lib/SalesCsv.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import regionReportSource from './lib/RegionReport.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>Yield from the handler. What it produces is streamed as it arrives, and the same handler
    still answers with the whole thing to anything that waits for the whole thing — one address, and
    no second endpoint to keep in step with the first.</p>

    <Section title="The three framings">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The handler returns</th><th>The caller gets</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>an <code>AsyncGenerator</code></td><td><strong>jsonl</strong> — one JSON value per line, <code>application/jsonl</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>jsonl(values)</code></td><td>the same, fixed: no <code>Accept</code> moves it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sse(values)</code></td><td><code>text/event-stream</code> — the same transcript, <code>data: </code> per line</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bytes(values)</code></td><td><code>application/octet-stream</code> — the chunks as they are, no framing</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Each takes <code>Values&lt;T&gt;</code>, and sse is reachable from jsonl without
        declaring it: send <code>Accept: text/event-stream</code> to a jsonl address and the answer
        is reframed, which is why it carries <code>vary: accept</code>. A helper fixes the framing,
        and nothing a caller asks for moves an answer off it. Every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/transports')}>Transports</a> reference.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An async generator streams as JSON Lines">
        <p>A handler returning an <code>AsyncGenerator</code> is framed as <strong>jsonl</strong> —
        one JSON value per line, <code>application/jsonl</code>, or
        <code>application/x-ndjson</code> for a reader that asks for NDJSON by name (16.44). The
        body is written per <code>pull</code>, so back-pressure reaches your database cursor rather
        than piling rows up in memory.</p>

        <p>That is the default framing and not the only one.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`sse` answers events whatever the caller asked for">
        <Example
            id="sse-wallboard"
            title="`sse(values)` — events, whoever asks"
            summary="A wallboard by the sales floor reads the visits feed. It sends no Accept header, and what comes back is events anyway."
            files={{
                'lib/VisitsFeed.abide': visitsFeedSource,
            }}
        >
            <VisitsFeed />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 helper, 0 lines of framing</em> — the arm writes <code>data: </code>, the blank
        line after it and the two headers a proxy needs to leave the stream alone, by hand, per
        event.</p>

        <p>The card's request carries no <code>Accept</code>, which is what a wallboard script or a
        <code>curl</code> sends, and without <code>sse</code> that caller is answered jsonl. A
        caller that does ask — an <code>EventSource</code> sends <code>Accept:
        text/event-stream</code> — gets the same events from a handler that never declared them: the
        jsonl transcript reframed as <code>data: &lt;json&gt;</code> per line with a blank line
        after it, <code>no-cache</code> and <code>X-Accel-Buffering: no</code>. That is one URL with
        two bodies, so it carries <code>vary: accept</code>. Declaring <code>sse</code> is for the
        caller who cannot say what it wants. The <code>:</code> the card's answer opens on is a
        comment, which sends the head before the first event exists.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`jsonl` sends a list a line at a time">
        <Example
            id="jsonl-export"
            title="`jsonl(values)` — a list sent one value per line"
            summary="The warehouse loads yesterday's sales a line at a time. The database hands back an array, and the export still goes out as lines."
            files={{
                'lib/WarehouseExport.abide': warehouseExportSource,
            }}
        >
            <WarehouseExport />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 helper, 0 joins</em> — the arm serializes each row and writes the newline after it,
        and a trailing one so the last line is whole to a reader that splits on them.</p>

        <p>An array is a value, so returned bare it goes out as one JSON array (16.30) — a loader
        that reads line by line gets one very long line. <code>jsonl</code> makes any
        <code>Values&lt;T&gt;</code> a stream, an array included, and fixes the framing: a caller
        sending <code>Accept: text/event-stream</code> is still answered lines (17.7).</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`bytes` writes a file as its rows are read">
        <Example
            id="bytes-csv"
            title="`bytes(values)` — a file written as its rows are read"
            summary="Sales by region as a CSV download. Each line goes into the file as the cursor reaches its row."
            files={{
                'lib/SalesCsv.abide': salesCsvSource,
            }}
        >
            <SalesCsv />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 helper, 0 stream controllers</em> — the arm hand-writes the
        <code>ReadableStream</code>: a pull per row, the header line first, the close at the
        end.</p>

        <p>Each chunk goes out as it is yielded, unframed, as <code>application/octet-stream</code>.
        A stream whose first chunk is binary is answered that way without the helper (16.47); what
        <code>bytes</code> adds is saying so before there is a first chunk. An export with no rows
        yields nothing to read the type from, and is answered as an empty jsonl stream rather than
        an empty file.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Every body helper takes `Values<T>`">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Values<T> = Iterable<T> | AsyncIterable<T> | ReadableStream<T>`} />

        <p>One input type across <code>page</code>, <code>jsonl</code>, <code>sse</code> and
        <code>bytes</code>. A source that is already a <code>ReadableStream</code> is not converted
        into an iterable to become one.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The caller gets a `Reactive`, not a stream to manage">
        <Example
            id="stream-rows"
            title={'`{#for row of call()}` — the rows, and the whole of them'}
            summary="Sales per region, a row at a time. Watch the list fill while the count still has nothing to say."
            files={{
                'lib/RegionReport.abide': regionReportSource,
            }}
        >
            <RegionReport />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 call, 0 line buffers</em> — the arm carries the tail of every chunk to the next
        one, a chunk being a read of the socket rather than a row.</p>

        <p><code>{'salesByRegion({ year })'}</code> hands back the same <code>Reactive</code> shape
        the handler had, chunks included (16.3). <code>rows</code> adopts it, so to a cursor, a
        guard and an await <code>rows</code> <strong>is</strong> that stream (11.15), and the cursor
        walks on the caller's side exactly as it did on the server's:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#for row of rows}'}</code></td><td>every row the run has produced so far, and each one after as it lands (9.1)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{rows}'}</code></td><td>the <strong>accumulation so far</strong>, materialized on the read that asks for it (9.1)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rows.busy()</code></td><td>true until the stream <strong>closes</strong>; <code>ready()</code> is true from the first chunk (3.7, 3.8)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await rows</code> on the value itself</td><td>the accumulation at close (2.7)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Nothing copies the accumulation per chunk (9.2), and a reader is woken at most once per
        task however many chunks landed in it (9.9).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/repeat-markup-over-a-list')}>Lists</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A reader arriving mid-run replays the run">
        <p>There is no retention option on the transport and none is owed. What a late reader needs
        is the answer being built, and a cursor over the stream hands it the run in flight from its
        start before it goes live (2.5). So a reader arriving halfway through still paints every
        row.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A stream is not a channel">
        <p>Both change more than once, and how the producer produces separates them. A channel is an
        array written by <code>patch</code>, and every patch leaves a whole array behind. A body
        that is or returns an async generator is a stream, yielding chunks of one value (9.6), and
        no chunk is a whole anything. That is why a stream is <code>busy()</code> until it closes
        (3.7), where a value with no producer is never busy (3.9).</p>

        <p>What they share is keying. A <code>{'{#for}'}</code> over a stream's accumulation is
        positional unless something names a key (32.13) — the block's own <code>by</code>, or a
        <code>by</code> the value declares (32.32) — and a row with an identity of its own says
        which:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/reports/index.abide — excerpt" code={`{#for region of salesByRegion({ year: 2026 }) by region.code}
    <tr><td>{region.name}</td><td>{region.total}</td></tr>
{/for}`} />

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page in flight is not held for an unfinished stream">
        <p>The document is <strong>not held</strong> for one — nothing holds it. What the stream
        produced by the time the walk finished is already inline; the client's own request picks the
        rest up from where the render reached, and the rows keep appending.</p>

        <p>Seeding holds one transcript whichever framing the caller asked for — the stream's own
        chunks, framed as jsonl or SSE when the browser's call takes them.
        <code>ABIDE_MAX_STREAM_BUFFER_SIZE</code> is the ceiling on one, counted in jsonl bytes, and
        an entry past it is dropped: the browser's call runs the handler again, and says so on
        <code>abide:hydrate</code>.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> ·
        <a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> — pushed
            rather than pulled, many callers on one channel</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/put-a-ceiling-on-a-request')}>Limits</a> —
            <code>timeout</code> is per chunk on a handler that yields</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
